WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.380
If you follow education, you're used to
seeing school reformers described as

00:00:09.389 --> 00:00:13.679
conservative. Meanwhile, education reform
advocates tend to describe their own

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:19.859
efforts as centrist. So which is it? Turns
out, it's neither. In a new study, we

00:00:19.859 --> 00:00:23.699
analyzed political contributions from
the staff at hundreds of the nation's

00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:27.900
leading school reform organizations, and
found out that they were overwhelmingly

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:33.000
Democratic by a margin of nine-to-one or
more. This rivals the leans we see in

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.090
famously Democratic precincts like
Hollywood. It turns out that reformers

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:43.610
are more uniformly democratic than is
the National Education Association! A

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:47.969
coalition so wedded to one political
party will inevitably suffer when it

00:00:47.969 --> 00:00:52.739
comes to forging alliances, finding
converts, or speaking to many parents and

00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:57.840
educators. Its members may not appreciate
or even much care how their proposals

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.280
and rhetoric are received by those to
their right. Put simply, a monochromatically

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.660
blue school reform movement is an
unhealthy one.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:08.720
Do you think school reform

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.080
can be bipartisan when politics is so
polarized? Let us know in the comments.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.610
Also, let us know what other topics you'd
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:23.780
and be sure to like and subscribe for
more research and videos from AEI.

